  "ExplanationCorrect": "The Degree of Financial Leverage (DFL) measures the sensitivity of EPS to changes in EBIT, driven by fixed financing costs (interest). DFL = EBIT / (EBIT − Interest) = %ΔEPS / %ΔEBIT. For Firm L: DFL = $16M / ($16M − $4.8M) = $16M / $11.2M = 1.43. This means EPS changes 1.43 times as much as EBIT: a 10% EBIT increase (to $17.6M) produces EBT = $17.6M − $4.8M = $12.8M, EPS = $12.8M / 5M = $2.56 — a 14.3% increase from $2.24. For Firm U with no interest: DFL = $16M / ($16M − $0) = 1.00 — EPS changes exactly proportionally with EBIT. The higher DFL for the levered firm means greater EPS volatility: shareholders in levered firms experience magnified gains when EBIT rises and magnified losses when EBIT falls. This is the financial risk that accompanies debt financing — it is distinct from business risk (operating leverage) and is fully borne by equity holders.",
