  "DifficultyScore": 3,
  "ExplanationCorrect": "In an efficient market, a share repurchase financed with excess cash should not change the stock price if the shares are repurchased at fair market value. Pre-repurchase: market cap = 25M × $32 = $800M, EPS = $62.5M / 25M = $2.50, P/E = $32 / $2.50 = 12.8×. Post-repurchase: shares repurchased = $40M / $32 = 1.25M shares, remaining shares = 23.75M. The firm gives up $40M in cash, so total firm value falls from $800M to $760M. Post-repurchase stock price = $760M / 23.75M = $32.00 — unchanged. Post-repurchase EPS = $62.5M / 23.75M = $2.632 — a 5.3% increase. The P/E ratio adjusts: $32.00 / $2.632 = 12.16× (lower). The EPS increase is mechanical (fewer shares dividing the same earnings) and does not represent value creation. The true economic effect: shareholders who tender receive $32 in cash per share; remaining shareholders own a larger percentage of a smaller company. The repurchase is neutral for shareholder wealth when executed at fair value — it is a return of capital, not a value-creation event.",
