  "ExplanationWrongC": "Share repurchases do not always destroy value. When a firm has excess cash that exceeds its positive-NPV investment opportunities, returning that cash to shareholders — through dividends or repurchases — is value-preserving. Retaining excess cash that earns below-market returns DOES destroy value (the agency cost of free cash flow). Whether the repurchase is value-creating, value-neutral, or value-destroying depends on whether the shares are repurchased above, at, or below intrinsic value. Repurchasing overvalued shares destroys value; repurchasing undervalued shares creates value.",
