  "ExplanationCorrect": "The forward premium or discount quantifies the percentage difference between the forward rate and the spot rate, annualized to a per-year basis: Premium/Discount = (F − S) / S × (360 / Days) × 100. = ($0.00918 − $0.0091) / $0.0091 × (360 / 90) × 100 = $0.00008 / $0.0091 × 4 × 100 = 0.008791 × 4 × 100 = 3.52% annualized. A POSITIVE result indicates a forward premium — the foreign currency (yen) costs MORE in the forward market than in the spot market. This means the market expects the yen to APPRECIATE against the dollar. Conversely, if the forward rate were below the spot rate, the result would be negative, indicating a forward discount (expected depreciation). The numerator convention (F − S) is critical: a higher forward rate than spot → premium (strengthening); a lower forward rate → discount (weakening). FX markets use a 360-day convention, not 365 days.",
