  "ExplanationCorrect": "The EBIT indifference point is the EBIT level where EPS is equal under two financing alternatives. For debt: EPS_debt = (EBIT − Interest)(1−t) / Shares_old. Interest = $50M × 7% = $3.5M. Shares remain 10M. For equity: EPS_equity = EBIT(1−t) / Shares_new. Shares increase to 12M. Set equal: (EBIT − $3.5M)(0.75)/10M = EBIT(0.75)/12M. Cancel 0.75: (EBIT − $3.5M)/10 = EBIT/12. Cross-multiply: 12(EBIT − $3.5M) = 10(EBIT). 12EBIT − $42M = 10EBIT. 2EBIT = $42M. EBIT* = $21M. At EBIT = $21M: Debt EPS = ($21M − $3.5M)(0.75)/10M = $17.5M×0.75/10M = $1.3125. Equity EPS = $21M×0.75/12M = $15.75M/12M = $1.3125. Since current EBIT ($40M) exceeds $21M, debt produces higher EPS: Debt EPS = ($40M−$3.5M)(0.75)/10M = $2.74 vs. Equity EPS = $40M(0.75)/12M = $2.50. The indifference analysis reveals that above $21M EBIT, the fixed interest cost of $3.5M is more than offset by avoiding the dilution of 2M additional shares.",
